I'm joined today by Malcolm Harris who's  a philadelphia-based freelance writer  and author of kids these days human  capital and the making of Millennials  I've been reading quite a bit lately  Malcolm about the topic of Millennials  it's a popular issue for people that are  Millennials and people that are not  Millennials to write about and there's  sort of like two general trends I see  two types of writing about Millennials  one is the very critical this is a lazy  generation this is a generation that is  not made up of makers but it's takers  etc that's one sort of line the other is  Millennials are a generation that like  no other are burdened by exploding  student debt stagnant wages a not  particularly great job market in the  sense that more and more education is  required even for jobs that previously  did not require such high levels of  education my instinct is the truth is  probably a combination of the two and  and many other things what is your sort  of general take on the existence of  Millennials in 2017 well it sort of goes  back to how you define a generation and  the way I'm looking at it in my book is  as this age cohort roughly between 1980  and 2000 but what's most important isn't  the year range it's how were responding  to trends and crises and the dominant  social forms that are occurring during  our lifetime so what I see is the the  main sort of shaping force in the  Millennial experience is the increasing  rate of exploitation the divergence  between average compensation and average  worker productivity and when you say  when you say that that that difference  we've looked at the minimum wage that  that we should have today had it tracked  inflation the minimum wage that we  should have today had it tracked  productivity since 1968 but what you're  saying is that it's a more acute  difference a more acute departure  between wages and productivity among  Millennials than among any other age  cohort absolutely well and as we watch  that trend in that divergence from the  60s and 70s 50s even it grows as we keep  going and so the leader you are as a  cohort in this development the more  you're going to experience it and the  more it's going to affect your  development as a worker but also as a  person as a citizen are there any other  characteristics of the modern economy  that you would look at in trying to  understand the place of Millennials  compared to older cohorts of workers  absolutely and there are a lot that I do  human capital development and its role  in education and job training is a huge  part of it a job polarization the growth  of bad jobs is a huge part temporary  work contracts the deregulation of  worker protections all of these things  are part of the same trend and a common  thread we can see in all of them is the  successful effort to push labor costs  down what are some ways in which the  Millennials that are succeeding are  succeeding that differ from or at least  are opportunities that only exist in the  2017 economy that did not previously  exist or or I guess what I'm saying is  in a different way  how have the more successful Millennials  use the unique characteristics of the  modern economy to sort of separate  themselves from the suppression of wages  well the best way is to own things and  so the more whether it's inherited  wealth in a lot of cases it is or owning  shares whether you you buy into a  business again most of this is not  through labor is the best way to succeed  in the 21st century economy is not to be  a worker at all it's to be a capitalist  and that involves owning things so the  what we call the successful Millennials  which is a small group a shrinking group  in fact  succeeds mostly by owning things when  you look at the cost of college and  what's going on with student lending and  the cost of college compared to wages  upon graduation is this even remotely  sustainable I I talked to friends all  the time about how can these trends  continue because even just 5 or 10 years  from now even the the the it seems as  though it would be completely outrageous  to even take on the debt required for  even something like a liberal arts  degree which statistically speaking it  is not going to really put you in a good  place to pay back those loans talk about  the role of what's going on with cost of  college in this situation the worry  actually for me having done the research  is that it is sustainable and that the  system can go on so well I'm serious  because one of the things we've seen  over time is the increase in value of  the college premium in wages so it makes  a bigger difference whether you go to  college or not that actually has not  been driven by gains in wages for  college educated but falling wages for  those with without a college education  and so it's fear that it's going to keep  driving people into college but unlike  the housing market where if you're over  leveraged on your house you can walk  away you can't do that when it comes to  human capital debt when it comes to  education debt there's no way to walk  away from the loans and so once you take  them out you will pay them back  eventually even the Treasury has to pry  it out of your social security checks  which they will do so the the system as  it exists can be sustained and can in  fact get much much worse without  collapsing is that what you see is  happening I mean do you do you not see  that there's going to be anything that  will either for political reasons or  economic reasons reverse this trend that  we're seeing of this explosion of not  only the the sort of retail price of a  college education but also the ease with  which one can get loans to cover those  exploded prices yeah I don't see any any  reversal happening we haven't seen that  it's not in the numbers we don't see any  sort of mechanisms any effective  attempts to drive down the cost of  attending college there have been some  attempts to sort of halt the rate of  increase which I think has to do more  with eating the peak of the ceiling of  what people can actually pay but it'll  keep going up I think it will continue  so economic circumstances aside do you  think there's any validity to some of  the other criticisms or at least  explanations that some put forward as to  why millenials are so different than all  prior modern generations for example  smartphone use that represents a sort of  different relationship to the device  than any previous generation had the age  of the internet for example a  globalization I mean you take take your  pick are there any of these more  cultural explanations that you think  hold water no I really don't Jeanne  twins another scholar and she's quoted  in the book about generational  difference although she's more of a  consultant these days and her book IGN  puts forward this idea that generation  the next generation be after Millennials  which she calls ijen it's also the name  of her consulting company that this  generation this cohort has been shaped  by cellphones directly and I read the  book that's that's really her whole  argument but the trends I'm looking at  go back to the late 70s early 80s the  economic trends and so we're not looking  at something that starts in in the 2000s  we're looking at something that started  decades ago with phenomenon that a lot  of commenters commentators like to call  neoliberalism these are the consequences  and phones have become a part of that  lifestyle part of the practice of  neoliberalism but we shouldn't confuse  it with the cause what do you see as  sort of the generational pass-through of  what's currently going on with  Millennials as more and more Millennials  now have kids and will be having kids in  the next three to five years  distally anyway how will the children of  Millennials be in  by what's going on I hope it's too soon  to tell because if we just extend the  trends of our experience further another  couple decades it gets really scary very  quickly and people are going to have a  hard time an increasingly difficult time  raising happy well-adjusted children  into this world into America if there is  some fundamental social political shift  in the next few decades which i think is  definitely a possibility then those  conditions change yeah I mean what  you're describing really should point to  Millennials being the most activist  minded generation in a long time given  how disproportionately some of these  issues are affecting Millennials is that  borne out in the data are we seeing that  aside from sort of what we've observed  for a long time which is that younger  generations tend to be more progressive  than older generations and that's sort  of how they vote are we actually seeing  a higher rate of voting among those who  are currently millennial aged than we  saw previously among the older  generations when they were millennial  aged I don't think it's that much about  voting  I think voting has become a a debased  form of political involvement and  yong-ki this younger cohort is skeptical  of that process also voting young people  voting youth voting is not that old 18  year-olds have not had the franchise for  many generational cohorts so I'm not  sure that's the best mode of  longitudinal comparison what would one  appoint you or what would you look at I  think we have seen a more politically  active active sophisticated cohort of  young people than we've seen in a long  time absolutely you know we look at the  the situation the political situation  the economic situation and think wow  there should be you know huge protests  of young people on every American city  this should be you know a nationwide  movement and there was we had Occupy  where that was a thing that happened  and that's what motivated it and since  then we've had a different kind of  discussion in this country in terms of  economic inequality in terms of how  capitalism works and young people more  and more are finding positions outside  the political mainstream that appeal to  them how are they turning that into  activism in the most or how could they  turn that into real-world change through  activism what types of active attactive  ism would be most successful and you've  already pointed out vote voting is not  it well if I had that answer  I'd be going doing that instead of  writing books I guess well you could be  doing both right I mean if you had if  you have an idea I'd be curious what it  is what would be the other avenues so  one of the things I do in the conclusion  of my book is look at a couple avenues  that were taught are the useful ways of  political engagement how we're supposed  to change our society whether that's  volunteerism or voting or the market or  protest demonstrative protest and I sort  of go through all four of those and show  how I don't think they can work in the  timeframe in which we need them to work  in this country I think we've got really  a limited a couple decades in which to  make really huge fundamental changes to  our society or else we're screwed or  else it doesn't matter and so what we  call it when the fundamental mode of  society is transformed is revolution and  so that's the kind of change that we  need in America I think we've been  talking about the book kids these days  human capital and the making of  Millennials the book's author is Malcolm  Harris Malcolm thanks so much for  talking to us about this thanks for  having me David

